# Line endings are the repository's business, not the checker's.
#
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true, and actions/checkout does
# not override it. Without this file every LF-only file in the tree arrives on a
# Windows runner as CRLF, and four tests that hold a byte-exact expectation
# about a file's contents fail with "want \n, got \r" — on a repository where
# nothing has changed any line endings. It also breaks scripts/test.sh's shebang
# and hands the embedded sample campaign to //go:embed with CRLF, so a campaign
# page round-trips differently on the machine that ships it.
#
# The rule is one line: text files are LF in the tree and LF in the repository,
# on every platform, and CRLF is something a file's *contents* decide — the
# reason the entries below are marked -text rather than converted.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Fixtures whose whole subject is what a checkout did to them. `crlf-mixed.md`
# exists to prove that a CRLF file round-trips byte for byte, and a checkout
# that normalises it turns a mixed file into a pure-CRLF one, so the test stops
# testing the case it was written for. Marking it -text leaves the blob exactly
# as committed, which is the only way a fixture about line endings can be tested.
internal/md/testdata/crlf*.md -text
